1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,314
This comparison tests Zeron and TerminalX in separate fresh macOS virtual machines
from the same Lume base.

2
00:00:06,314 --> 00:00:15,177
Both used the same Codex version and local fixture, without provider credentials.
TerminalX was a development build; Zeron was a published release.

3
00:00:15,177 --> 00:00:25,986
The video separates observed behavior from source-confirmed features. Narration is
synthetic. Different capture geometry means these images are not a controlled
density or performance benchmark.

4
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:34,835
Zeron starts with a guided sequence: choose an appearance, decide whether to sync,
install an agent, then open a project.

5
00:00:34,835 --> 00:00:39,366
Sync can be skipped, so local use does not require an application account.

6
00:00:39,366 --> 00:00:48,151
A microphone permission prompt appeared before voice was requested. TerminalX should
borrow the useful guidance while requesting permissions when the feature is invoked.

7
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:54,826
Clicking Install beside Codex started an inline installation and updated the
provider row when it finished.

8
00:00:54,826 --> 00:01:03,044
This is a concrete onboarding advantage: the next step remains in the product
instead of requiring the user to find a shell and installation instructions.

9
00:01:03,044 --> 00:01:08,057
Installed did not mean authenticated. The subsequent task still needed a provider
login.

10
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:16,270
The fresh TerminalX screen offers a local project, a GitHub project and quick start.
Application sign-in is optional.

11
00:01:16,270 --> 00:01:21,247
A missing-agent notice links to Claude Code, Codex, re-check and agent settings.

12
00:01:21,247 --> 00:01:28,618
This is a useful fast path for existing command-line users. A guided readiness
checklist would help someone starting from an empty Mac.

13
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:37,386
TerminalX agent settings show installation commands and external links. Get it
opened the official Codex documentation in Safari.

14
00:01:37,386 --> 00:01:46,752
After the test operator installed the matching standalone binary, re-check correctly
detected Codex and showed its path. This footage begins after that installation.

15
00:01:46,752 --> 00:01:53,556
The new composer nevertheless initially selected missing Claude Code. Prefer an
available provider when the default cannot start.

16
00:01:54,760 --> 00:02:00,921
Zeron keeps project, device, model, reasoning level, branch and checkout close to
the prompt.

17
00:02:00,921 --> 00:02:05,461
The checkout picker makes current checkout versus a new worktree an explicit
decision.

18
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:14,193
For both products, the user should be able to explain which agent runs, where it
runs, what files it changes and which permissions apply before starting.

19
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:26,395
TerminalX exposes model, effort, permissions, worktree and automation choices. Its
model list refreshed to include GPT-6.1 Sol; the test selected High effort.

20
00:02:26,395 --> 00:02:35,095
Both apps received the prompt: read readme dot m d. TerminalX used the requested
bypass mode in the disposable fixture, with automation switches off.

21
00:02:35,095 --> 00:02:40,027
The improvement is clearer grouping and readiness, rather than adding controls
already present.

22
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:48,148
The prompt created a real Zeron worktree. A separate shell opened there, and print
working directory verified the location.

23
00:02:48,148 --> 00:02:53,644
Because Codex was signed out, the chat eventually showed an authentication error
with a login action.

24
00:02:53,644 --> 00:02:59,529
This gives the user a specific reason for the failure, although a preflight
readiness check could have caught it sooner.

25
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:08,666
TerminalX also created the worktree, initially showed Working, and then reported
unconfirmed prompt delivery after about twenty-eight seconds.

26
00:03:08,666 --> 00:03:12,823
The recovery notice suggested checking the terminal and possibly pressing Enter.

27
00:03:12,823 --> 00:03:21,390
The actual prerequisite was provider sign-in. Recognizing that state would give the
user a direct setup action instead of generic delivery troubleshooting.

28
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:29,604
Switching the agent tab to terminal revealed the real Codex process waiting for
login. No provider credentials were supplied.

29
00:03:29,604 --> 00:03:36,440
This shared chat and terminal process is a TerminalX strength: users can inspect
exactly what the agent is waiting for.

30
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:44,088
A separate shell tab also opened in the worktree and passed the same working-
directory check. Agent terminal and ordinary shell are distinct tools.

31
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:51,462
The test operator deliberately added three README lines to exercise the diff. This
was not an agent-generated edit.

32
00:03:51,462 --> 00:03:56,891
Zeron showed the working-tree scope, added-line count and expandable diff beside the
conversation.

33
00:03:56,891 --> 00:04:03,835
The file tree and Markdown preview were also exercised. These are practical
workspace capabilities to compare directly.

34
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:11,100
TerminalX opened the README in Markdown preview, switched to source, and saved the
same three-line addition.

35
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:14,650
Independent Git readback confirmed three additions and no deletions.

36
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:24,665
At this VM’s narrow width, sidebar and tool panels reduced the document area.
Improve focus and tool discovery while preserving the working editor and existing
pane controls.

37
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:34,274
The runtime inspection corrected an early source-only hypothesis: TerminalX already
displays scope, target branch and merge-base information.

38
00:04:34,274 --> 00:04:40,901
Its scope menu offers All changes, Uncommitted changes and Last turn. Only All
changes was exercised here.

39
00:04:40,901 --> 00:04:49,317
The improvement list therefore does not propose adding missing scope labels. Mixed
staged, untracked and turn-specific changes need a separate test.

40
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:58,120
Zeron loaded a public page inside its workspace browser. Switching to dark
appearance also changed the embedded page’s presentation.

41
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:02,866
Its neighboring file, terminal and diff surfaces keep the task context close.

42
00:05:02,866 --> 00:05:10,030
The test establishes navigation and appearance behavior, not browser performance or
full application-preview compatibility.

43
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:20,923
TerminalX loaded the same public URL in a workspace browser tab, with a live preview
and an Open window action. The CLI confirmed the URL and workspace association.

44
00:05:20,923 --> 00:05:24,054
Its interaction model differs from Zeron’s inline browser.

45
00:05:24,054 --> 00:05:32,015
A controlled Settings open-and-close test retained the selected browser. Command-
palette search also found the README, session and workspace.

46
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:39,440
TerminalX’s dashboard placed the blocked session in Needs you. That is useful when
work spans several projects.

47
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:43,956
The routing worked; the explanation should name provider setup when that is the
blocker.

48
00:05:43,956 --> 00:05:50,671
Later, Stop changed the session to Stopped and removed the live Codex process.
Stopped is not the same as completed.

49
00:05:51,880 --> 00:06:01,911
TerminalX already offers four themes, light and dark modes, transcript text sizing,
layout options and editable shortcuts. Dark appearance survived reopening.

50
00:06:01,911 --> 00:06:07,631
Zeron adds accent, material and separate font controls, with theme import confirmed
in source.

51
00:06:07,631 --> 00:06:17,567
Prioritize measured contrast, focus, text scaling and narrow-window usability. These
screenshots do not establish accessibility compliance for either product.

52
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:29,349
Zeron’s Appshots setup describes a global capture shortcut, destination, optional
accessibility text and review before sending. Capture itself was not enabled in this
test.

53
00:06:29,349 --> 00:06:34,748
TerminalX could connect floating chat and attachments into a user-triggered capture-
to-draft workflow.

54
00:06:34,748 --> 00:06:43,239
Voice and transcription controls exist in both products, but no audio was recorded.
Dictation should remain distinct from live voice conversation.

55
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:53,074
Zeron defines ten production harness identities in the inspected release. TerminalX
offers Claude Code and Codex, with legacy adapters hidden.

56
00:06:53,074 --> 00:07:02,483
Only Codex was installed and attempted in these walkthroughs. Additional providers
need reliable authentication, status, interruption, resume and permissions.

57
00:07:02,483 --> 00:07:09,894
Both projects also implement remote capabilities. Local settings inspection does not
establish cloud or multi-device parity.

58
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:20,896
The lifecycle test terminated only the TerminalX desktop process, then reopened it.
The local runtime and waiting Codex process retained their process identities.

59
00:07:20,896 --> 00:07:29,428
The app reconnected, and the saved diff, dark mode and shell tab remained. It asked
for a session selection; the previous browser tab was absent.

60
00:07:29,428 --> 00:07:37,562
Restore recoverable workspace and browser context. This was a process-termination
test, not a verified normal Quit or full reboot test.

61
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:43,084
TerminalX’s real agent terminal and attention dashboard were observed directly.

62
00:07:43,084 --> 00:07:51,777
Its issue-to-pull-request workflow, independent review and commit-bound merge gates
are documented in source and were not exercised against GitHub in these VMs.

63
00:07:51,777 --> 00:07:58,567
Preserve these strengths. A pull-request viewer and a gated review pipeline
represent different levels of workflow support.

64
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:07,720
Start with guided installation, available-provider selection and truthful readiness
states: missing, signed out, ready or unknown.

65
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:15,689
Make unconfirmed delivery explain the last confirmed stage and a safe next action.
Preserve the draft through login and avoid duplicate retries.

66
00:08:15,689 --> 00:08:28,223
Next, restore workspace context after a desktop restart and simplify launch and tool
discovery. The report gives evidence, priorities, effort estimates and acceptance
criteria for fourteen proposals.

67
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:42,351
The local comparison covers fresh setup, signed-out launches, worktrees, shells,
files, diffs, browsers and settings. TerminalX also received attention, stop and
desktop-restart checks.

68
00:08:42,351 --> 00:08:52,772
Neither product completed authenticated coding. Cloud, sync, voice, Appshots
capture, production updating and performance rankings remain outside the verified
scope.

69
00:08:52,772 --> 00:09:00,578
Both VMs are stopped and retained. The report and evidence ledger let you trace each
recommendation to an observation or an exact source version.
